{
  "configuration_id": "config_021",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_027 (scgpt + imagenet_vit_l, concat, singleprofile) is the best result so far (acc=0.4348, F1=0.4234), modestly outperforming RNA-only scgpt (config_005, acc=0.4106) and weighted-fusion multimodal (config_051, acc=0.4224). However, all three experiments used scgpt as the RNA encoder, so we have zero information about whether the RNA representation choice is a meaningful design axis. Question: config_021 (scvi_n200 + imagenet_vit_l, concat, singleprofile) is a single-variable controlled comparison to config_027—only the RNA encoder changes from scgpt to scvi_n200. This isolates the effect of the RNA encoder while holding image encoder, fusion, and profile setting constant. Why this is the best next experiment: Under a tight budget, the RNA encoder is a fundamental design decision that could account for substantial performance variance. If scvi_n200 matches scgpt, we learn the RNA representation is not a bottleneck and can focus budget on other axes (aggregation, multiprofile, image encoder). If it differs substantially, we gain a critical design insight that reshapes the remaining search. This is more informative than, e.g., testing an image-only baseline or jumping to multiprofile, because it directly extends the strongest evidence we currently have with a minimal, interpretable change."
}

